WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:01.610 align:start position:0%
 
well we can speak now to dr. Oliver  Johnson the program director for the  Kings Sierra Leone partnership he  returned from Freetown Sierra Leone  just this morning Oliver thank you very  much indeed for being with us you've  returned for a short period and when you  came into the country  what kind of screening did you go  through this morning well the first  thing to say the screening started  before I came into the country even as  they left Freetown Airport there was a  detailed history taken my temperature  was checked three times by a health  worker and then arriving at Heathrow we  had was another temperature check and a  detailed history with a nurse there who  took a picture of everyone who was  coming out of West Africa which is  interesting because exit screening is  something that has been talked about a  lot in Europe today and there's been a  lot of international supervision of  airports in West Africa to make sure  they're up to scratch so I think what we  can say is it's gulia you know robust  intervention there to make sure Britain  remains safe but what we've got to  remember as well is that to stay safe  it's got to be about tackling the  response at the heart of the outbreak in  West Africa we can't just put up walls  what we've got to do is reduce  transmission and stop the outbreak right  back in Sierra Leone and Liberia Guinea  where the cases are being transmitted  from it just in terms of the screening  and you got to Heathrow there was more  screening for you today and what kind of  monitoring do you go through now so it's  different for every patient and so what  they'll or every passenger rather so for  every passenger they'll work out their  level of risk and they'll decide you  know whether they need to be followed up  or whether or not just to give them good  health information and good you know  maybe regular phone calls to take  temperatures this sort of thing so it's  a robust practice that's one we're  seeing across the world at the moment  and it's yeah I think it's very  appropriate and you're confident of that  practice and the protocols that have  been put in place are the right ones  yeah I mean I defer to the specialist of  Public Health England who are the real  experts on this what I really know is  it's Sierra Leone but for me I know I  was impressed tonight there's a medical  expert with what you know about how  Ebola can be contracted you're confident  yeah I mean it seemed very appropriate  to me in terms of the experiences that  you've had in Sierra Leone you've been  out there for two years now so you were  out there at the point at which the  Ebola crisis took hold what has it been  like for you I think when we first heard  that there was a bola in the region what  we expected would was we'd see maybe a  dozen cases or a couple of dozen where  the King's team is based in in Connaught  Hospital in downtown Freetown we serve a  small unit what we've actually  experiences is far beyond what any of us  could have imagined hundreds so I think  we you know from day one we were  starting to get trained MSF was there  the World Health Organization and Kings  brought in infectious diseases  specialists in UK who'd managed a bola  before so we were really doing our best  to get ahead of the game but the country  of the whole you know its health system  wasn't prepared for waters has been a  tsunami of new patients and what we're  seeing at the moment is an exponential  increase that means that every day there  are more new cases than there were the  day before there's a desperate effort at  the moment of which Kings are the  central part to try and set up more beds  so we can isolate patients in from the  community and stop people transmitting  into their homes but at the moment we're  still playing catch-up and I know the  British governments you know the whole  effort of the British Army the Foreign  Office differed in other organisations  is to get that bed capacity up so that  we can turn this outbreak around and  begin to reduce the number of cases what  are conditions like now working in there  and have they changed over the past few  months as the world has woken up to the  threat of Ebola I think the world did  wake up late at one point you know Kings  was one of the only organizations  alongside mid senza frontiere whu-oh who  were there what we're now seeing is a  major response to a lot of funding and a  lot of organizations now flying in more  medical experts more supplies the  challenge really is to find  organisations willing to go on the  frontline a lot of organizations are you  know for doing training and other things  but to really have the clinical  specialists he'll go  shoulder-to-shoulder with Sierra Leonean  colleagues who are fighting this on the  frontline is the challenge and what we  need now is people like that and people  from across the NHS to come forwards so  that we can get hospitals ready  hospitals opened and begin to you know  take the challenge back to this disease  and be on the front foot again and there  are many like you who will say this is  my job this is my duty this is what I  want to do but in the knowledge that you  are putting yourself at risk I've been  overwhelmed by the quality and the sort  of self-sacrifice and confidence of the  people like my team I've got about 10  doctors nurses pharmacists from the NHS  who have taken time out to come as  volunteers to really you know step up  and they are you can minimize the risk  but no organization can never eliminate  it and so absolutely people who are out  there they're aware of the risks but  they're putting themselves in danger not  only because they want to make sure that  we protect our colleagues in in Sierra  Leone and and the patients there but  also to keep britain safe but seeing  what you see and the suffering that you  see day after day after day on the scale  that we're seeing with ebola what impact  does that have  I think for all of us it's it's it's a  horror what we're seeing out there it's  it's the modern-day plague for the whole  city of Freetown which I know the city  is completely changed it's it's a quiet  city now and there's a tension that  everyone feels everyone's afraid and for  doctors and nurses that people are my  team we've had colleagues be medevac  flown out other colleagues seralini and  colleagues who have died and we've been  the ones to treat them every day now  we're seeing whole families young  children aunties uncles grandparents all  come in many of them died and sometimes  just one or two family members left and  desperate pleas from communities who say  look I've got a sick relative at home  can't we you know bring them into  hospital but there simply aren't enough  beds leading to really horrible choices  and so it's so challenging very  challenging time and we're seeing  situations now where children have been  orphaned and they're also being  distanced from their own communities  because of the fear that they might have  contracted or be carrying the virus you  know what we tried to do is to really  demonstrate that the confidence that  survivors should feel by you know when  they leave hugging them helping them  back into their communities to try and  reduce the stigma but the stigmas  everywhere at the stigma is also felt by  our health workers in the hospital  Sierra Leonean colleagues but two  fantastic young nurses there who have  been told by their uncle where they live  you can't come home if you continue to  work in that unit and they've said well  we're committed to this it's we're doing  this for our people in our country so  they now live in the hospital with us  because they can't go home and many of  the British health workers when they  come home they stigma come you know you  can't have you felt that coming home you  know I have had I've got a family and  friends who have welcomed me back but I  know others have felt it hard to pin  said you can't come to this event or you  know you know welcome here or we're  afraid of touching you and I think that  there is a real sense now of fear in the  UK as well about these also  things and from the you know the British  government I think the decision to stop  the direct flights by Gambia bird from  London to Freetown is it it's a  devastating blow to the response and  it's a it's really disappointing that  the government has made that decision we  had urgent medical supplies and urgent  new volunteers who would do for those  flights but I think there's a lot of  fear now amongst in the government and  so sometimes I think sometimes over  reactions okay dr. Oliver Johnson thanks  very much indeed for talking to us and  good luck when you return to Sierra  Leone thank you

